  {"id":"hint-switchyard","targets":["switch-to-bit","quiz-switchyard"],"levels":[{"level":1,"text":"Before the foreman went back to his paperwork he worked the belt in front of you. Each switch on it settled into one of two places. Cast your mind back to that demonstration before you turn to the gauge.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"failed-attempts:quiz-switchyard","value":1}},{"level":2,"text":"Those two places are called on and off: a line carries a mark or it carries none. Now read the switch gauge. Its needle rests against a stamped line, and that line is the marking you need. Hold both ideas together and see what the needle has to say.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-switchyard","value":1}},{"level":3,"text":"Work one switch at a time. Set it, watch where the needle settles against the stamp, and write down the mark it names. Repeat until the belt agrees with you, then give the foreman your own answer.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-switchyard","value":2}}],"autoOfferAfter":1,"cooldownTurns":2},
  {"id":"hint-data-types","targets":["data-types","quiz-data-types","puzzle-sort-the-data-types"],"levels":[{"level":1,"text":"Look at the five labelled bins in the hall and the single loose sample beside them, the one with no type name on it. Each bin keeps one kind of value; it is the loose sample you must file into one of them. Decide what sort of thing each bin will accept before you put the sample anywhere.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"turns-since:puzzle-sort-the-data-types","value":3}},{"level":2,"text":"The boards name exactly five types: Boolean, character, integer, real and string. Boolean holds a True/False condition, character a single number, letter or symbol, integer a whole number, real a number with a decimal point, and string a run of characters. Five bins, five kinds of value, each with its own label; compare the sample's own characteristic against them and make the choice yourself.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-data-types","value":1}},{"level":3,"text":"Now file the sample yourself. Ask one question at a time: does it show a True/False condition, a single mark, a whole number, a number with a decimal point, or a run of marks? Look closely at whether the sample carries a decimal point, then match that against the five bin labels. Put it in the bin whose description fits; one of the five is its home.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-data-types","value":2}}],"autoOfferAfter":3,"cooldownTurns":2},
  {"id":"hint-number-systems","targets":["number-conversion","quiz-number-systems","puzzle-set-the-switches","puzzle-add-with-carries","puzzle-shift-the-row","puzzle-read-nibbles"],"levels":[{"level":1,"text":"Before you convert anything, look at the columns of beads on the carry track and the groups of squares on the nibble card. Each position has a weight based on the base, and the rightmost is the smallest. Let that tell you what a digit in a place is worth.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"turns-since:puzzle-set-the-switches","value":3}},{"level":2,"text":"Write a place-value row under the digits: powers of two for binary, powers of sixteen for hexadecimal. On the nibble card the squares are grouped in fours, so work from the right and fill one group at a time. A hexadecimal digit takes exactly one group of four beads.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-number-systems","value":1}},{"level":3,"text":"Add from the rightmost column leftwards. In each column add the two bits and any carry already waiting. Follow the rule card: 1 + 1 gives zero and carries one; with a carry already there, the column reads one and still carries one. Pass each carry into the next column before you move on.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-number-systems","value":2}},{"level":4,"text":"For a shift, draw the row before and after you move it and count how many columns it has. A bit pushed past the last column drops into the tray scratched OVERFLOW under the carry lamp instead of vanishing; a bit brought in at the other end has to come from somewhere. Say whether the value grows or shrinks, but leave the exact figure to your own working.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-number-systems","value":3}}],"autoOfferAfter":3,"cooldownTurns":2},
  {"id":"hint-representation","targets":["character-representation","graphics-representation","sound-representation","quiz-character-representation","quiz-graphics-representation","quiz-sound-representation","puzzle-match-characters","puzzle-build-mosaic","puzzle-tune-sampling"],"levels":[{"level":1,"text":"Every room here keeps its content as many small pieces. Find the smallest piece first: one mark on a tile in the character case, one square in the mosaic, one measurement in the choir. Hold that piece in view before you begin to count.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"turns-since:puzzle-match-characters","value":3}},{"level":2,"text":"The labels supply the formal terms. A single mark is a character and a run of them is a string; the stamp names ASCII and Unicode as standardised character sets. In the mosaic, resolution is the width and height in pixels, bit depth is the bits used for each pixel's colour, and colour depth is measured in bits per channel (bpc). At the desk, a sample is one measurement at a point in time; sample rate counts samples per second, bit depth counts bits per sample, and bit rate multiplies sample rate by bit depth.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-representation","value":1}},{"level":3,"text":"Take one tile at a time and hold its mark against the character list on the stamp: is that exact mark in the set you are checking? The spare drawer holds a tile labelled 'a different character' — test it the same way rather than assuming from its label. File a tile only after you have compared its mark.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-representation","value":2}},{"level":4,"text":"For the mosaic, multiply width by height for the pixels, then multiply by the bits used per pixel — the bits per channel times the number of channels. For the choir, multiply sample rate by bit depth by channels by seconds. Both answers arrive in bits; change unit one step at a time, dividing by eight for bytes and then by 1024 for KB, and stop when the unit matches what was asked. The machinery hands you no ready total; the figure is yours.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-representation","value":3}}],"autoOfferAfter":3,"cooldownTurns":2},
  {"id":"hint-storage-capacity","targets":["data-storage-units","quiz-storage-units","puzzle-stack-the-bytes"],"levels":[{"level":1,"text":"Look at the eight rungs on the shelves and at the brass plate. Each rung names an amount of storage and has a neighbour above and below. Notice what the joint between two rungs is marked before you convert anything.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"turns-since:puzzle-stack-the-bytes","value":3}},{"level":2,"text":"Write the chain in order: bit, nibble, byte, kilobyte, megabyte, gigabyte, terabyte, petabyte. A nibble is four bits and a byte is eight bits. From the byte upward the unit ladder marks each joint ×1024, and the plate states one kilobyte holds 1,024 bytes. Set each multiplier beside its joint.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-storage-capacity","value":1}},{"level":3,"text":"Move one rung at a time; never skip a joint. Going up, multiply by the factor marked on that joint; going down, divide by it. Those factors are powers of two, so reach for 1024, not 1000 — the stack keeper counts by the marked factors and has no time for a decimal answer. Write each step down until the unit matches the one requested.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-storage-capacity","value":2}}],"autoOfferAfter":3,"cooldownTurns":2},
  {"id":"hint-compression-ratio","targets":["data-compression","quiz-data-compression","puzzle-pack-the-archive"],"levels":[{"level":1,"text":"The press is meant to make a file smaller while leaving it usable. Two settings are cut into the die: lossless at one end, lossy at the other. Before you choose, decide what 'usable' means for the file in your tray.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"turns-since:puzzle-pack-the-archive","value":3}},{"level":2,"text":"In the glass window one column is labelled original and the other compressed. Write both sizes down and be clear which is the starting size and which is the result: the ratio compares original against compressed. Note whether the first tray returns the file exactly or the second comes back a little blurred — that is what separates the two settings.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-compression-ratio","value":1}},{"level":3,"text":"Form the ratio as original divided by compressed, then cancel it down as you would a fraction. If a size is missing, rearrange it: compressed equals original divided by the ratio, and original equals compressed multiplied by the ratio. At the lever, justify your setting by asking whether this file must return exactly or may lose a little quality, and say why. The figure and the choice are yours.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-compression-ratio","value":2}}],"autoOfferAfter":3,"cooldownTurns":2},
  {"id":"hint-pattern-bureau","targets":["algorithms-and-pseudocode","quiz-pattern-bureau"],"levels":[{"level":1,"text":"The Loomwright's counter is where the demonstration runs. The card on it shows one mill pattern written two ways at once: lines of pseudo-code down the page, and the same routine drawn as a flowchart. Read the paper once for the run of it, then again line by line before you commit to anything. The shapes stencilled into the Bureau's woodwork are the flowchart's five working symbols, and the demonstration can be heard again whenever you ask.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"failed-attempts:quiz-pattern-bureau","value":1}},{"level":2,"text":"Sit with the card and say what each line is for. Pseudo-code runs one instruction to a line, top to bottom, and the flowchart tells the same story in shapes: a rounded end to begin and end it, parallelograms where bobbins or figures come in and go out, rectangles for the work done between them. A diamond is a question the cloth must settle before the run goes on.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-pattern-bureau","value":1}},{"level":3,"text":"A diamond asks its question and both of its exits matter: one arm runs, the other waits, and the cloth must not walk both. When the quiz asks what the pattern will produce, walk the arrows the loom would, from the rounded start to the rounded end, and let each shape say the one thing shapes say; then say what sits on the rail at the end, in your own words, one figure to a line.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-pattern-bureau","value":2}}],"autoOfferAfter":1,"cooldownTurns":2},
  {"id":"hint-threading-order","targets":["programming-constructs","puzzle-dress-the-warp"],"levels":[{"level":1,"text":"The Warp Dresser hangs the steps in the order the pattern numbers them. Sequence is the mill's whole case here: every step present, every step after the one that must precede it, and none before.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"turns-since:puzzle-dress-the-warp","value":3}},{"level":2,"text":"Sequence is the formal name; the dressing order is how the mill says it. Read the pattern paper's numbers first, then hang each shade card to match its number, ground to first stripe to cross-stripe to border to hem, and let no later step jump the queue.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-threading-order","value":1}},{"level":3,"text":"Order is kindness in this room: a card hung out of turn is worn by the whole cloth. When every hanger carries the number the paper gives it, the Warp Walk counts the threading proof and lights it.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-threading-order","value":2}}],"autoOfferAfter":1,"cooldownTurns":2},
  {"id":"hint-colour-court","targets":["programming-constructs","puzzle-lift-the-shed"],"levels":[{"level":1,"text":"The court routes every pick urgent, and not one of them says routine on its face. Selection is the work here: a condition lifts some threads and drops the rest, and no pick runs both arms of a branch. The shed plate wants its diamond slot and the else plate its plain rectangular one; neither arm runs until both plates sit home.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"turns-since:puzzle-lift-the-shed","value":3}},{"level":2,"text":"The plate's condition chooses, and the pick obeys it. A pick that satisfies the condition takes the if arm; a pick that fails it takes the else arm; and a pick offered where no arm waits is filed with no arm at all. Read the condition before you route the tray's picks 9, 12 and 15 — the numbers are the court's rehearsals, not the cloth itself.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-colour-court","value":1}},{"level":3,"text":"Return the shed plate to its diamond slot and seat the else plate in the plain rectangular one, then route the tray by the plate's condition, one pick at a time, before you call the court done. The arm a pick takes is the condition's decree, never the pick's wish — and the avalanche stays armed until the discipline holds.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-colour-court","value":2}}],"autoOfferAfter":1,"cooldownTurns":2},
  {"id":"hint-weft-tally","targets":["programming-constructs","puzzle-seat-the-ratchet"],"levels":[{"level":1,"text":"The Weft Tally counts everything, including her own passes and Tuesdays, and a bound of ten means ten to the pattern, no more, no fewer. Ask her to count aloud again before you run the loom, and let the chalk on the bailing bench show what the bound divides: whole bundles only, the remainder posted over.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"turns-since:puzzle-seat-the-ratchet","value":3}},{"level":2,"text":"Count-controlled iteration keeps its promise with an incremental count: each pass asks one more of itself, and the bound stops the asking. The slip tooth — the cousin's addition — has been telling the ratchet otherwise, which is why the loom cannot hold ten.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-weft-tally","value":1}},{"level":3,"text":"Pry the slip tooth from the counting ratchet before you run the loom again, then re-seat the ratchet so one tooth serves one pass. Make your prediction to the Weft Tally BEFORE the run: seat the bound at ten, and let chalk say what the remainder does — the bundles a pattern posts are whole ones, never the drift a tooth slips in.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-weft-tally","value":2}}],"autoOfferAfter":1,"cooldownTurns":2},
  {"id":"hint-selvedge","targets":["programming-constructs","puzzle-close-the-selvedge"],"levels":[{"level":1,"text":"The corridor-loom weaves its edge on and on because the exit plate wants its until back: a run of repeats with no ending is a queue without a desk. The counterfoil drawer at the Warden's end of the room holds paperwork that ends a loop; the rogue thread is a signal, not a datum, and filings go where filings go.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"turns-since:puzzle-close-the-selvedge","value":3}},{"level":2,"text":"Condition-controlled iteration runs the body first and asks the condition after — while the cloth is nice is how the cousin left it, which is no condition at all. The until plate restores the bound; the rogue thread, the dyed colour that never arises in this pattern, reads −1 and goes to the counterfoil drawer, filed like any other signal the mill cannot weave.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-selvedge","value":1}},{"level":3,"text":"Set the condition plate so the loom asks its question at the end of each pass, and file the rogue thread where signals are kept — both halves, or the selvedge never ends and the whist evening at the back of the weft never closes either. The edge stops because the work says so; the Warden's patience, budgeted as it is, thanks you.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-selvedge","value":2}}],"autoOfferAfter":1,"cooldownTurns":2},
  {"id":"hint-finished-rail","targets":["algorithms-and-pseudocode","quiz-finished-rail"],"levels":[{"level":1,"text":"The rail's rule is PREDICT. THEN WEAVE. — the rail will not despatch until a prediction is on record, and neither will the quiz behind it. Say what the final cloth will read, in your own words, before you ask the mill to weave it.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"failed-attempts:quiz-finished-rail","value":1}},{"level":2,"text":"The master cloth joins the four mended procedures: sequence lays the ground, selection lifts by condition, the tally's bound holds at ten, and the until plate ends the edge — one algorithm drawn twice, once in pseudo-code, once in shapes, input to output in a straight run. The joining is the synthesis: each construct is the same algorithm seen from a different room.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-finished-rail","value":1}},{"level":3,"text":"Walk the questions one at a time: for each, find the construct that carries it — sequence, selection, a bound of iteration, or the input–output seam itself — and say what each shape commits before you commit with it. The rail stamps no cloth it has not seen predicted; the mill is the butt of its own machinery, never the learner's, and the rail holds to that bargain even now.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-finished-rail","value":2}}],"autoOfferAfter":1,"cooldownTurns":2},
  {"id":"hint-bobbin-window","targets":["programming-constructs","puzzle-mend-the-bell"],"levels":[{"level":1,"text":"The window's trade runs both ways, bobbins in and cloth out, and just now neither way says the right thing. What comes in should be announced by the intake bell, and the bell has hung silent since the cousin wrote his note about focus. What goes out should be addressed by the despatch plate, and its addresses have stopped reading as places. Stand at each end of the window and ask what that instrument is for, and what it would take to do its work again.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"turns-since:puzzle-mend-the-bell","value":3}},{"level":2,"text":"Two fixtures carry the window's two jobs, and each wants its own action. The bell's hook is empty: the wire with the bell-shaped tag lies behind the Weft Counter's pigeonholes, a walk away, and it fits that hook exactly — a bell without its wire cannot announce what arrives. The plate needs no new part, only its letter rack worked: its strip was read through one find-and-replace, so each address now carries a word it never earned, and every letter is still in the rack, waiting to be put back. Neither fixture mends by being looked at, and the Bobbin Lad mends neither for you.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-bobbin-window","value":1}},{"level":3,"text":"Carry the wire to the window and seat it on the intake bell's hook, so the instrument can speak again; then work the plate's rack one address at a time. Where a word stands that no place answers to, read the letters around it, decide which word the pass replaced, and set that name back letter by letter until the line reads as a place again. Work the rack through every address before you call the trade open — the last name, like the rest, is yours to read.","unlock":{"kind":"counter-at-least","key":"hints-shown:hint-bobbin-window","value":2}}],"autoOfferAfter":1,"cooldownTurns":2}
